Q = {
'YA-1-1': [
  'A coworker says, “Arguments for God never prove anything, so belief is just a leap in the dark.” Which reply best reflects Catholic teaching?|Reason has no part in faith, so the coworker is right to treat belief as a leap|The arguments are designed to force every honest person to believe in practice|Reason can point to a source of all things, while faith answers God who reveals|Reasoning about God matters only to philosophers, not to ordinary believers|2|CCC 31-36 says reason can reach God as the source of all, and CCC 50-53 says revelation completes what reason begins.',
  'Why does the Church call natural knowledge of God a “doorway” to revelation rather than a replacement for it?|Reason reaches a first cause, but the Trinity and Gospel had to be revealed|Reason is unreliable about God, so only revealed truth can be trusted at all|Revelation makes the earlier reasoning about God obsolete once it is received|Natural knowledge is identical to revelation but simply less detailed in content|0|Reason can know that God exists, but what God has made known of himself in Christ lies beyond what reason alone reaches (CCC 50-53).',
],
'YA-1-2': [
  'A friend explains the Trinity as “one God who shows up sometimes as Father, sometimes as Son, sometimes as Spirit, like one actor in three masks.” What is the problem?|It claims there are three separate gods who simply cooperate on tasks|It makes the Son and the Spirit lesser creatures made by the Father|It presumes the Trinity can be proven by reason without any revelation|It denies that the Persons are really distinct and eternally related|3|This is modalism (Sabellius), rejected because the Father, Son and Spirit are really distinct Persons (CCC 253-256).',
  'Why does it matter for ordinary faith that God is a communion of Persons and not one solitary Person?|It shows that God needed creation in order to have someone to love as a rule|Love is then what God eternally is, not something begun after creation|It explains how three separate divine beings share the work of salvation|It lets believers set aside the mystery as a puzzle that they can solve|1|“God is love” (1 John 4:8) is true from eternity because the Father, Son and Spirit give themselves to one another (CCC 221, 257).',
],
'YA-1-3': [
  'Someone says, “Jesus was a wonderful moral teacher, but no more.” Which observation most directly challenges that view?|He taught love of neighbor, which other great teachers also taught well|He forgave sins and claimed oneness with the Father, as no mere teacher could|He worked miracles, which proves only that he was a prophet like others|He was executed, which shows his contemporaries rejected his moral message|1|His hearers understood these claims as claims to divinity (Mark 2:5-7; John 10:30-33), so “teacher only” does not fit the Gospels (CCC 430, 441-445).',
  'Why does Chalcedon insist that Christ has a real human mind and will, not only a human body?|The divine nature is incomplete until it is joined to a human mind|His human will was meant to oppose and then yield to the divine will|It distinguishes the Son from the Father in power and in glory|Without a full human nature, he could not truly share and heal ours|3|Christ is one divine Person with two complete natures, so he truly shares our humanity (CCC 464-469).',
],
'YA-2-1': [
  'A reader notices that Genesis 1 and modern cosmology describe origins very differently and concludes that Scripture is unreliable. Which is the better Catholic response?|Ask what the author intended in that form, as Scripture teaches saving truth|Conclude that the human authors added mistakes that God did not intend|Insist that the text be read as literal science if it is really inspired|Set Genesis aside as a myth with nothing real to teach about creation in practice|0|Interpreters must attend to the intention of the human author, his genre and his time (CCC 109-110); the Bible teaches truth for salvation (CCC 107).',
  'Why do Catholics call the human writers “true authors” rather than secretaries taking dictation?|God dictated each word, bypassing the abilities and the situation of the writers|Their personal holiness guaranteed that everything they wrote was error-free|God used their own gifts and style, so the words are fully theirs and his|Later councils edited their texts to remove whatever the writers got wrong|2|CCC 106: God chose men who made full use of their own faculties, so that they wrote all and only what he wanted.',
],
'YA-2-2': [
  'A friend says, “If it is not in the Bible, Catholics just made it up as Tradition.” What is the best answer?|Tradition is a second book of extra teachings added after the Bible was done|Tradition is whatever customs the Church finds helpful in any given century|Tradition is the apostles’ teaching handed on, from the same source as Scripture|Tradition lets the Church change what Scripture says when times change in the long run|2|Scripture and Tradition flow from the same divine source and form one deposit of faith (CCC 80-82); Tradition is not a separate set of extra teachings.',
  'Which statement about papal infallibility is accurate?|It guards definitive teaching on faith and morals, not personal sinlessness|It means every statement a pope makes is free from error of any kind in most cases|It means the pope can define new doctrines unrelated to what was handed on|It means that a pope never needs Confession because he is preserved from sin|0|Infallibility is narrow and serves the deposit of faith (CCC 889-892); it is not impeccability.',
],
'YA-2-3': [
  'A friend says, “I follow Jesus, but I find the Church full of hypocrites, so I will go my own way.” Which reply is most faithful to Catholic teaching?|The Church has no real sins, so the friend is judging unfairly in most cases|Hypocrisy in members proves that the Church is not truly his Body as a rule|Personal prayer is enough, so the friend loses nothing by staying away|The Church’s sins are real, yet Christ chose to be present through her|3|The Church is holy in Christ yet made of sinners (CCC 827), and Christ gives himself to us through her (CCC 774, 790).',
  'Which of these shows the pattern of Church life seen in Acts 2:42-47?|Private study, personal devotion, solitary fasting and quiet reflection|Teaching, fellowship, the breaking of the bread and the prayers shared together|Public debate, political campaigns, charitable fundraising and publicity|Strict rules, mutual correction, financial audits and clerical oversight|1|The first community devoted itself to the apostles’ teaching, fellowship, the breaking of bread and the prayers (Acts 2:42).',
],
'YA-3-1': [
  'A busy parent says, “My mind wanders constantly when I pray, so I must be failing at it.” Which response is wiser?|Stop praying until the mind is calm enough for prayer to be real in practice|Distraction is normal and not itself sin; keep returning to God each day|Treat each distraction as a sin to confess before praying again|Switch to praying only when strong feelings of devotion are present|1|Distraction becomes a problem only if we cling to it; the remedy is to turn back to the Lord (CCC 2729-2733).',
  'Why does the Catechism say prayer is a relationship and not a performance?|Prayer works well only when we have found the right wording|Prayer is mainly a method for calming ourselves and clearing our minds|Prayer measures how holy we are by how much we feel as a rule|God thirsts for us first, and prayer is our answer to him|3|CCC 2560: God thirsts that we may thirst for him; prayer is the encounter of two thirsts.',
],
'YA-3-2': [
  'Someone reads a whole chapter of Scripture quickly each morning but feels nothing. Which change best fits lectio divina?|Read a short passage slowly, linger on a phrase and answer God plainly|Read even more chapters each day so that more of the Bible is covered|Skip reflection and move directly to a resolution for the day in most cases|Look up commentaries until the passage’s meaning is fully settled|0|Lectio divina aims at meeting a Person, not finishing a text: read, reflect, respond, rest (CCC 2705-2708).',
  'Why does the Church advise testing insights from prayer with Scripture with the Church’s own reading?|The Church must approve each insight before anyone may act on it, at least for a time|Personal insights from prayer are usually produced by the imagination alone in practice|Private impressions can err, and Scripture is read within the Church’s Tradition|Only clergy and scholars are able to understand Scripture correctly in practice|2|Scripture is to be read within the Church’s Tradition and the unity of the whole (CCC 111-114); a wise guide helps test what we hear.',
],
'YA-3-3': [
  'Two Catholics attend the same Sunday Mass. One says, “I just watch and wait for it to be over.” The other says, “I offer my week at the altar with Christ.” What best explains the difference?|The first is not really present, since Mass counts only if you feel moved|The second adds to Christ’s sacrifice what it lacked on the Cross for the most part|The second joins his own life to Christ’s offering, which is praying the Mass|The two differ in style only, since attention affects nothing at Mass in most cases|2|We are united to Christ’s one sacrifice and offer ourselves with him (CCC 1368-1369); our faith and attention open us to its fruit (CCC 1128).',
  'On a day when you feel dry and distracted at Mass, what is true?|Christ’s action does not depend on your mood, though faith opens you to its fruit|The Mass has little effect until your devotion is strong again, at least for a time|The Mass is only a symbol that depends on how it is celebrated for the most part|Your attendance does not count unless you feel prayerful the whole time in practice|0|The sacraments work by Christ’s action (ex opere operato, CCC 1128), while the fruit depends on our disposition.',
],
'YA-4-1': [
  'A man says, “I do not feel bad about missing prayer for a month, so I cannot have sinned seriously.” What does Catholic teaching suggest?|A sin is grave only when it causes strong guilt at the time it occurs|Missing prayer can never be sinful because prayer is not commanded in practice|Only acts that harm another person can be sins at all, at least for a time|Feeling is not the test; gravity, knowledge and consent decide seriousness|3|Mortal sin requires grave matter, full knowledge and deliberate consent together (CCC 1857-1859); how we feel does not settle it.',
  'Why does the Church say sin is more than breaking rules?|Rules exist only to protect the Church’s authority over believers|It is a failure of love that wounds God, ourselves and others|Sin matters only when it harms someone other than the sinner|Rules are the final standard, and love adds nothing to them|1|CCC 1849: sin is a failure in genuine love for God and neighbor, and wounds the nature of man.',
],
'YA-4-2': [
  'A man who has not been to Confession for twenty years is afraid to go because he cannot remember everything. What is the best advice?|Wait until you can recall every sin in its exact number and detail as a rule|Go, say it has been a long time, and confess what you remember honestly|Skip Confession and ask forgiveness in private prayer alone in practice|Confess only the small sins first and leave the serious ones for later|1|Honest confession of what one remembers is enough, and the priest guides the penitent (CCC 1456, 1483).',
  'A woman is sorry only because she fears punishment, not out of love for God. Can she still receive absolution?|No, only sorrow that arises from love of God is acceptable|No, fear shows she does not truly intend to change her life|Yes, but only if her sorrow is later made perfect before absolution|Yes, imperfect contrition from fear or shame is still enough|3|Contrition from fear is imperfect yet still sufficient in the sacrament (CCC 1453).',
],
'YA-4-3': [
  'A woman prays the Our Father daily but refuses to forgive her brother, saying, “God understands.” What does the lesson suggest?|Jesus ties God’s forgiveness to ours, so refusing contradicts her prayer|The prayer concerns only forgiveness from God, not toward others as a rule|Forgiving is optional if the hurt was serious enough, at least for a time|God forgives only after the brother apologizes and makes amends in most cases|0|Jesus adds, “If you forgive men their trespasses, your heavenly Father also will forgive you” (Matthew 6:14-15; CCC 2838-2845).',
  'Forgiving a person who abused you most accurately means which of the following?|Forgetting the harm and restoring the relationship as it was before|Telling yourself that the harm was not really so serious in practice|Letting go of revenge, wishing them well, and still seeking safety|Staying in contact so that they can see you hold no grudge|2|Forgiveness does not require excusing, forgetting or remaining in danger (CCC 2843-2845).',
],
'YA-5-1': [
  'A friend says, “Sacraments are just symbols; the grace comes from my own sincerity.” What is the Catholic response?|They give grace automatically, whatever the person’s disposition may be|They give grace only when the minister is personally holy in most cases|They truly give the grace they signify through Christ, received by faith|They are helpful reminders, but they do not themselves give grace in most cases|2|Sacraments work through Christ (ex opere operato), and their fruit depends on our disposition (CCC 1127-1128).',
  'Why does the Church use water, oil, bread and wine in the sacraments?|Since God became flesh, he gives grace through material signs|Spiritual things are best taught through simple ordinary objects|Physical things are needed because people cannot grasp spiritual ideas|These items were chosen by the early Church to attract converts|0|Because the Word became flesh, Christ continues to act through visible, material signs (CCC 1115-1116, 1146).',
],
'YA-5-2': [
  'A friend says, “When Jesus said ‘this is my body’ he obviously meant it as a symbol.” Which point best supports the Catholic reading?|Jesus normally spoke in symbols, so this saying would also be one|The earliest Christians treated the meal as a simple memorial in the long run|Many disciples stayed with him after the Bread of Life discourse in practice|Paul warns that unworthy reception wrongs the body and blood of the Lord|3|1 Corinthians 11:27-29 treats the eating as more than a symbol; John 6 shows disciples leaving over his words.',
  'What does the Church mean when she says the appearances of bread and wine remain after the consecration?|The bread and wine are still bread and wine but only represent Christ as a rule|The substance becomes Christ’s Body and Blood while the senses still see bread|Christ is present only while the bread is being received in Communion in the long run|Science can detect the change in the elements under close examination in practice|1|Transubstantiation names a change of substance while the appearances remain (CCC 1376).',
],
'YA-5-3': [
  'A woman baptized as an infant says, “Baptism was just something my parents did.” What does Catholic teaching say?|It only becomes effective once she chooses to affirm it as an adult|It gave her a lasting identity as God’s child, to be lived out|It wore off during years of not practicing and must be repeated|It was a welcome ceremony that carries no lasting spiritual effect|1|Baptism imprints an indelible character (CCC 1272-1274); the baptized are called to live as priest, prophet and king.',
  'Which statement about Confirmation is accurate?|It is a graduation from religious education that completes learning|It is optional and gives the Spirit to those who did not have him in the long run|It can only be received in adolescence by those prepared in school in practice|It seals the baptized with the Spirit for witness, and adults may receive it|3|Confirmation completes Baptism and strengthens for witness (CCC 1285, 1302-1305); adults may be confirmed.',
],
'YA-6-1': [
  'A friend says, “The Church teaches that the body is shameful, which is why it demands chastity.” What is the better reply?|The body is good and expresses the person; chastity guards love|The body is good, but only marriage makes sexuality lawful|The body is a temporary vessel, so what it does matters little|Chastity is a teaching for the unmarried, not for the married|0|CCC 2332-2337: chastity integrates sexuality into the person; it protects self-gift and is for everyone.',
  'How can a single person or a married person both live chastity?|Married people are free from it, since sexual union is permitted to them|Single people are called to it only until they eventually marry|Each lives it in their own state, as self-giving and faithful love|It means avoiding every kind of affection that involves the body|2|Everyone is called to chastity in their state of life (CCC 2348-2349).',
],
'YA-6-2': [
  'A man has been dating for a year and avoids talking about the future because “it might ruin the mood.” What does the lesson suggest?|Dating is primarily enjoyment, so serious questions should wait for engagement|Asking about the future signals that he does not trust the relationship|Dating is for discernment, so asking where this is going is part of loving well|Prayer alone will reveal the answer, without any conversation needed in the long run|2|Dating is a time to know whether marriage is possible (CCC 1632, 2350) and requires honesty.',
  'Which of these is a warning sign in a relationship?|One partner controls contact with friends, keeps secrets, and uses guilt to push boundaries|The partners disagree at times about how to spend their weekends, at least for a time|The partners keep physical boundaries decided in advance with a friend, at least for a time|One partner prefers group settings during the early part of dating, at least for a time|0|Control, isolation, pressure and secrecy are red flags; love respects the other’s freedom.',
],
'YA-6-3': [
  'A friend confides that he keeps relapsing into pornography and feels too ashamed to go to Confession. What is the most Christian response?|Tell him to quit through willpower before he goes to Confession|Tell him that it is minor and not worth taking seriously|Keep your distance until he proves he has changed his habits|Reassure him that mercy is for this, and encourage honest help|3|Shame keeps people hiding; mercy brings them into the light (CCC 1468; Romans 8:1).',
  'Why does the Church call pornography a grave offense?|It creates images that are secretly forbidden by civil law as a rule|It uses persons as objects and wounds the dignity of all involved|It inflames emotions that need to be strictly suppressed in most cases|It is thought to reduce attendance at church over time in most cases|1|CCC 2354: pornography offends against chastity because it perverts the conjugal act and gravely offends the dignity of those involved.',
],
'YA-7-1': [
  'A young man delays every major decision, waiting for a clear sign of God’s one right plan. What does the lesson suggest?|He should wait until a sign removes every doubt about the choice|Discern with prayer and counsel; the greater risk is never choosing|He should choose whatever he wants, since God has no preference|He should consult only himself, because the call comes in private|1|God’s will is a call to love along many faithful paths, discerned over time with counsel (CCC 2690).',
  'What is the first vocation of every Christian, whatever their state of life?|Parish service, which can be joined by every baptized person in most cases|Family life, which forms the first community of faith, at least for a time|Ministry, which belongs to every member of the Church, at least for a time|Holiness, the call to live in friendship with God and love of neighbor|3|All the faithful are called to holiness before any particular state (CCC 825, 2013).',
],
'YA-7-2': [
  'A couple plan to marry but privately intend never to have children. How does Catholic teaching see this?|Marriage is ordered to children, so excluding them undermines valid consent|It is fine, since the couple’s love is the only essential element|It is fine as long as they receive the sacrament in church in most cases|It only matters if they decide it after the wedding day, at least for a time|0|Consent must include openness to children, fidelity and permanence (CCC 1601, 1625-1632).',
  'What does an annulment declare?|That a valid marriage has ended and the couple may remarry in most cases|That the children of the marriage are no longer legitimate|That a valid marriage never came to be, due to defective consent|That the couple’s years together were sinful and meaningless as a rule|2|An annulment is a finding that the marriage was never valid, not a Catholic divorce (CCC 1629).',
],
'YA-7-3': [
  'A single woman feels her life is “a waiting room” until she marries. Which perspective from the lesson helps most?|A single life is a short-term arrangement that has no calling in it|Only those who join a religious order live a full vocation|Single life lived in love and service is a genuine vocation|Waiting is itself the vocation, so she needs to do nothing else|2|The single life given to God is a genuine way of loving and serving the Church (CCC 897-900).',
  'How does the Church understand the relationship between consecrated life and marriage?|Each is a real path to holiness, and consecrated life is a special sign of the Kingdom|Consecrated life is superior in holiness and marriage is merely natural in the long run|Marriage is superior because it adds children to the Church, at least for a time|They are identical callings that differ only in daily routine, at least for a time|0|The Church esteems virginity for the Kingdom while teaching that marriage is a sacrament and a path to holiness (CCC 1619-1620).',
],
'YA-8-1': [
  'A man works eighty hours a week and says, “It is for my family’s future,” though he is rarely home. Which question from the lesson is most helpful?|Is this the highest-paying job in the field he could take as a rule?|Does his employer approve of his level of commitment as a rule?|Is the work respected by people in his community as a rule?|Does this work serve people and leave room for God and family?|3|Work is for the person, not the person for work (CCC 2426-2428), and it must leave time for rest and worship.',
  'Why does the Church say work has dignity even when it is humble or tiring?|Because all jobs earn the same wage in a just economy for the most part|The worker is made in God’s image and shares in creation by working|Because tiring work is a penance that earns merit for heaven|Because the dignity belongs to the product that is made as a rule|1|Work’s dignity rests on the worker’s dignity (CCC 2426-2428).',
],
'YA-8-2': [
  'A comfortable family says, “We worked for what we have, so what we give is up to us entirely.” How does Catholic teaching respond?|Private property is unjust and should be given up to the poor as a rule|Property is good, but it carries a duty because goods are for all|Giving is only a charitable bonus with no claim of justice|Only the very wealthy are bound by duties toward the poor in practice|1|Private property is legitimate yet subordinate to the universal destination of goods (CCC 2402-2406, 2446).',
  'Which is the better approach to personal budgeting in light of the lesson?|Give whatever is left at the end of the month, if anything remains|Give only in emergencies, when the need appears obvious|Avoid budgeting altogether, since trust in God replaces planning|Make giving to the poor a regular line of the budget|3|Almsgiving is part of discipleship and justice (CCC 2443-2447); regular habits build generosity.',
],
'YA-8-3': [
  'A town council wants to decide how a small local school is run. Which principle suggests the decision be left to the local community?|Subsidiarity, which holds that decisions belong at the lowest competent level|Solidarity, which holds that every person is responsible to all others|The common good, which holds that all must share all decisions, at least for a time|Preferential option, which holds that the poor decide all matters in practice|0|Subsidiarity keeps decisions close to those affected (CCC 1883-1885).',
  'What is the best description of the Church’s role in politics according to the lesson?|She endorses the party closest to the Gospel and expects loyalty in practice|She stays out of public life and limits herself to private devotion as a rule|She teaches principles and forms consciences, but does not endorse a party|She tells members exactly how to vote on every issue, at least for a time|2|Catholic social teaching challenges every ideology; applying principles is left to prudence (CCC 2420, 2442).',
],
'YA-9-1': [
  'A friend whose child is very ill asks, “How can a good God allow this?” What is the wisest first response?|Explain that suffering always brings a greater good in the end|Tell her that God has a reason, so she should trust it|Be present and listen, rather than offering a tidy explanation|Remind her that suffering is the result of sin for the most part|2|Job receives God’s presence, not an argument; the first response to a sufferer is presence.',
  'Which statement about redemptive suffering is consistent with Catholic teaching?|It can bear fruit with Christ, but is never sought or used to excuse injustice|Suffering must be sought out because it automatically pleases God in the long run|Suffering has no meaning, and endurance is only a matter of will for the most part|Those who suffer abuse should accept it as part of God’s plan for the most part|0|Sufferings joined to Christ’s can be redemptive (Colossians 1:24; CCC 1521); Christians must also relieve suffering and protest evil.',
],
'YA-9-2': [
  'A student worries that learning about the Big Bang and evolution will cost him his faith. What is the best advice?|Reject any scientific claim that seems to conflict with Genesis|Treat Genesis as a myth with no truth to teach, at least for a time|Keep science and faith apart in his mind and never compare them as a rule|One God authors both, so sound science cannot truly contradict faith|3|CCC 159: there can never be any real discrepancy between faith and reason.',
  'Which claim goes beyond science and is not itself a scientific result?|The universe has changed over billions of years|Only what science can measure is true|Species have developed over long periods of time|Natural events can be studied by tested methods|1|Scientism is a philosophical position, not a scientific finding.',
],
'YA-9-3': [
  'A Catholic says, “Since the Church respects other religions, it does not really matter what anyone believes.” What is the right response?|That is correct, since all religions lead to the same destination in practice|The Church respects what is true in them but proclaims Christ as sole Savior|The Church rejects everything in other religions as false, at least for a time|Respect is shown by avoiding the subject of Christ altogether for the most part|1|Nostra Aetate 2 and CCC 843-846: the Church rejects nothing true and holy, yet proclaims Christ.',
  'What best describes the Catholic approach to dialogue with other Christians?|Treat other Christians as unbaptized and as outside salvation as a rule|Downplay differences in doctrine to reach quick agreement in practice|Avoid contact to protect the faith from confusion for the most part|Seek unity through prayer and honest talk, honoring the bond of Baptism|3|Other baptized Christians are in imperfect communion with the Church (CCC 818-819), and unity was Christ’s prayer.',
],
'YA-10-1': [
  'A man says, “I pray and read Scripture, so I do not need a community or a mentor.” What does the lesson suggest?|Faith is lived with others who know us, challenge us and keep us faithful|He is right, because private prayer is the heart of the faith as a rule|He needs a community only if he faces a period of doubt in the long run|He should join any group that is willing to welcome him for the most part|0|We are made for communion, and the Church is where faith grows (Hebrews 10:24-25; CCC 1878-1880).',
  'Which is a warning sign of an unhealthy community?|It asks members to commit time each week to meetings, at least for a time|It holds members to a clear standard of belief and conduct as a rule|It discourages questions and contact with family, and demands secrecy|It sometimes challenges members to change harmful habits as a rule|2|Authority serves, and the fruits of healthy community are freedom, truth and charity.',
],
'YA-10-2': [
  'A woman wants to share her faith with a coworker who is not religious. Which approach best fits the lesson?|Argue persuasively until the coworker admits the faith is true|Wait until she is an expert in theology before saying anything|Befriend and listen, live so that questions arise, then invite|Leave religious tracts on the coworker’s desk each week|2|Evangelization starts with witness and friendship and is always an invitation, never a trap (1 Peter 3:15-16; CCC 160).',
  'Why is it wrong to pressure someone into faith?|Faith is a free response to God, and it cannot be forced|Pressure rarely works as quickly as gentle persuasion does|It is the job of clergy, not lay people, to invite others|People should simply be left to find faith on their own|0|CCC 160: to be human, man’s response to God by faith must be free.',
],
'YA-10-3': [
  'A man makes a rule of an hour of prayer, daily Mass, fasting and weekly service, and quits within two weeks. What does the lesson advise?|Set the rule even higher so that quitting becomes harder to do|Drop the idea of a rule, since grace alone sustains faith in practice|Keep the full rule and treat each failure as a sin to confess as a rule|Start small, add one habit at a time, and begin again without drama|3|A rule is a trellis for grace, not perfection; start small and review it with a friend or director.',
  'What is the danger of legalism in a rule of life?|The rule becomes so flexible that nothing really happens|The rule becomes the goal and replaces loving God and neighbor|The rule leads others to think you are better than they are|The rule requires help that busy people cannot find in the long run|1|The goal is love, not perfection; the rule serves grace.',
],
'YA-11-1': [
  'A friend says, “If the pope is infallible, he cannot sin or be wrong about anything.” What is the correct response?|He is correct, since the pope is preserved from error in all matters|It is narrow, covering definitive teaching on faith and morals only|Infallibility applies only to the pope’s decisions on Church discipline|Infallibility is a title and does not protect any teaching in the long run|1|Infallibility covers definitive teaching on faith and morals (CCC 889-892), not impeccability or opinion.',
  'Which is an example of authentic development of doctrine, not change?|The Church reverses an earlier teaching because culture has shifted|The Church adds a new revealed truth that was unknown to the apostles|The Church drops a teaching once it becomes hard to accept in the long run|The Church states more clearly what was always believed about Christ|3|Doctrine develops in understanding without changing in meaning (CCC 66, 94); Nicaea clarified, not invented.',
],
'YA-11-2': [
  'A student says, “The Church has always been right, so those stories about slavery must be exaggerated.” What does the lesson suggest?|Members of the Church sinned gravely, yet popes and saints spoke against it|The Church has done only harm, and nothing good can be said of her in the long run|Only a few individuals sinned, and the Church itself has not erred in the long run|The Church’s past is irrelevant, since the Gospel stands apart as a rule|0|The Church is holy in Christ yet always in need of purification (CCC 827); truth includes both failure and witness.',
  'What does the Church teach about responsibility for the death of Jesus?|The Jewish people of that time bear collective responsibility|The Roman authorities alone are to be held responsible in most cases|Sinners are responsible, and Jews are not collectively guilty|No one bears any responsibility, since it was God’s plan as a rule|2|CCC 597-598 and Nostra Aetate teach that guilt cannot be imputed to the Jewish people as a whole.',
],
'YA-11-3': [
  'A Protestant friend says, “Asking Mary to pray for you takes away from Jesus.” Which reply fits Catholic teaching best?|Mary is a second mediator who adds to what Christ has done in most cases|Mary is worshiped, as God alone is, because she is his mother as a rule|Mary’s intercession shares in his mediation, like any friend’s prayer|Asking for prayers is optional and has no biblical foundation as a rule|2|Honor given to Mary differs from the worship due God alone (CCC 971, 2096); her role leads to Christ (CCC 969).',
  'What does the Immaculate Conception teach?|Mary was kept free of original sin from her conception, by Christ’s grace|Jesus was conceived without a human father in Mary’s womb for the most part|Mary was taken body and soul into heaven at the end of her life|Mary remained a virgin before, during and after the birth of Jesus as a rule|0|The Immaculate Conception concerns Mary’s own conception (CCC 490-493); it is not about the virginal conception of Jesus or the Assumption.',
],
'YA-12-1': [
  'A Catholic finds a Church teaching hard and says, “My conscience tells me it is fine to ignore it.” Which response is most faithful?|Follow conscience without further study, since it is always right as a rule|Obey the teaching silently and ignore the doubts completely in practice|Conclude that the teaching is outdated because it feels wrong|Conscience must be formed, so study, pray and ask a wise guide first|3|We must obey a certain conscience, yet we are responsible for forming it (CCC 1783-1785, 2039).',
  'When is a Christian justified in refusing to obey a civil authority?|Whenever the law is inconvenient or costly to follow as a rule|When it commands something morally evil, since God comes first|Whenever the person disagrees with the policy behind the law|Never, since the state’s authority comes from God in practice|1|Acts 5:29 and CCC 2242: citizens are obliged in conscience not to follow directives contrary to the moral order.',
],
'YA-12-2': [
  'A voter says, “Since no party matches Church teaching perfectly, a Catholic should not vote.” What does the lesson suggest?|The voter is right, since no Catholic can take part without compromise|Participation is a duty; form conscience on all the principles|Catholics should vote for the party that the bishops officially endorse|Catholics should vote on a single issue and disregard the rest|1|Participation is a duty of citizenship (CCC 2240), and the Church teaches principles without endorsing a party.',
  'Which attitude toward fellow Catholics who vote differently fits the lesson?|Assume those who disagree have rejected the faith for the most part|Avoid discussing politics with them to protect your own views as a rule|Insist that they change their vote before sharing a table at church|Differ in good faith on means, avoid contempt and keep friendship|3|Catholics may differ on prudential applications; contempt violates charity (CCC 2477-2479).',
],
'YA-12-3': [
  'A man who prayed fervently for years now finds prayer empty and says, “God has left me.” Which perspective fits Catholic teaching?|Dryness is common, and fidelity in prayer through it can deepen love|Dryness shows that he has lost faith and should stop praying|Dryness is a punishment that will continue until he repents|Dryness is a sign that prayer is no longer needed, at least for a time|0|Teresa of Calcutta and John of the Cross show faith as fidelity, not feeling (CCC 2726-2728).',
  'A woman has been persistently hopeless and sad for months and calls it a dark night. What does the lesson advise?|Treat it only as a spiritual trial to be endured in prayer in practice|Assume it is a sign of weak faith and pray harder, at least for a time|Seek a doctor or counselor as well, since dryness and depression differ|Wait until it passes before telling anyone close to her in most cases|2|Faith and healthcare are partners; hopelessness warrants medical help.',
],
'YA-13-1': [
  'A parishioner arrives at Mass in time for the offertory, saying the readings are just a warm-up. What is wrong with this?|Nothing, since only the Eucharistic part of Mass is required in most cases|Attendance counts only when the homily has been heard, at least for a time|In the readings Christ speaks to his people; both halves are one worship|The first half is optional preparation provided by the priest|2|The two parts of the Mass form one act of worship (CCC 1346-1349).',
  'Why does the Creed follow the homily?|It is the people’s response, professing what they have heard and believe|It summarizes what the priest said in the sermon, at least for a time|It tests the faith of anyone who wishes to receive Communion in practice|It replaces the psalm that was prayed after the first reading in the long run|0|The Creed is the assembly’s answer to the Word proclaimed (CCC 1349).',
],
'YA-13-2': [
  'A visitor says, “At Mass the priest is just reenacting the Last Supper as a drama.” What does the Church teach?|It repeats the sacrifice, so Christ is offered up again each time|It is only a symbolic memorial that helps people recall the Supper|It adds to what Christ accomplished on the Cross, at least for a time|It makes Christ’s one sacrifice present, so it is real and not a drama|3|The sacrifice of the Cross and the Eucharist are one sacrifice (CCC 1364-1367).',
  'Why does the Church kneel or bow at the consecration?|Christ is present only symbolically, to be honored with respect|Christ is truly present, body, blood, soul and divinity|The priest is acting in his own power to change the gifts|The gestures are traditions that carry no special meaning|1|CCC 1374-1378: the Eucharist is adored because Christ is really present.',
],
'YA-13-3': [
  'A man is aware of a mortal sin he has not confessed but joins the Communion line to avoid attracting attention. What does the Church teach?|He should receive, because God knows his heart and forgives all|He should go to Confession first, as Communion requires grace|He should receive, since staying seated would only cause scandal|He should wait for a month and then receive in the Easter season|1|CCC 1385, 1415: anyone aware of mortal sin must receive the sacrament of Reconciliation before Communion.',
  'What does the dismissal “Go in peace” invite Catholics to do?|Leave quietly so that others may pray in peace after Mass|Carry the sacred vessels to the sacristy in procession in practice|Rest for the day and think about the homily, at least for a time|Live what they have received as the Body of Christ in the world|3|The Eucharist commits us to the poor and sends us on mission (CCC 1397).',
],
'YA-14-1': [
  'A man with a heart condition is awaiting major surgery and thinks the Anointing of the Sick is only for the dying. What is the right answer?|He may, before serious surgery, and again if his condition worsens|He may not, because it is reserved for those at the point of death|He may, but only once in his life, so he should wait for the most part|He may if a deacon is present, who can administer it in most cases|0|CCC 1514-1515: the sacrament is for serious illness or before surgery, and it can be repeated.',
  'What does the Church promise about the effects of the Anointing of the Sick?|Full bodily healing for any person who has enough faith in the long run|Forgiveness of sins only, without any aid to the body for the most part|Strength, peace and union with Christ’s Passion, and healing if God wills|A guarantee that the person will recover from the illness in most cases|2|CCC 1520-1523: grace of strength and peace, forgiveness of sin, and healing when God wills.',
],
'YA-14-2': [
  'A man says, “I will live as I like and sort it out in the next life.” Which teaching most directly answers him?|There is a second chance after death to choose again, at least for a time|Everyone ends in heaven in the end, whatever their choices as a rule|Each person is judged at death, so choices made in this life matter|Judgment occurs only on the last day, not at death, at least for a time|2|CCC 1021-1022: each person receives eternal retribution in the particular judgment at death; there is no reincarnation (CCC 1013).',
  'What best describes the Church’s teaching about hell?|It is real: a freely chosen, definitive exclusion from communion with God|It is a symbol for the pain of this life, with no actual existence|It is a punishment God imposes on those he never wanted to save in most cases|It is a state that the Church has confirmed for certain named individuals|0|CCC 1033-1037: hell is self-exclusion, God wills all to be saved, and no individual has been declared damned.',
],
'YA-14-3': [
  'A woman asks, “If my father is in purgatory, am I wasting my time praying for him?” What does the Church say?|Yes, since purgatory is fixed and cannot be changed by the living|No, though only the saints in heaven can help those in purgatory in practice|Yes, as he is already in heaven and does not need our prayers|No, Mass, prayer and mercy help the dead through the communion of saints|3|CCC 1032, 1371: the Church offers prayer, almsgiving and above all the Eucharist for the dead.',
  'Which statement about purgatory is accurate?|It is a second chance for those who rejected God in life as a rule|A final purification of the saved who are not yet ready to see God|It is a temporary hell where the unrepentant suffer for a time|It is a neutral waiting room where souls remain until the last day|1|CCC 1030-1031: those who die in God’s friendship but imperfectly purified are assured of salvation.',
],
'YA-15-1': [
  'A reader is disturbed by violent passages in the Old Testament and says the God of the Old Testament is not the God of Jesus. Which response fits the lesson?|Remove those passages from the Bible, as Marcion proposed|God teaches gradually; read these passages in the light of Christ|Treat every such passage as a direct command for today for the most part|Read them only as symbols with no historical element for the most part|1|CCC 53, 122: God’s teaching is gradual; Marcion’s rejection of the Old Testament was condemned.',
  'Which sense of Scripture comes first, and why?|The allegorical sense, since it connects the text to Christ|The moral sense, since it tells us how to act now for the most part|The anagogical sense, since it points to the final goal as a rule|The literal, since the other senses rest on the author’s meaning|3|CCC 116: all other senses of Scripture are based on the literal.',
],
'YA-15-2': [
  'A reader notices that the Gospels tell some events differently and concludes they are unreliable. What does the lesson suggest?|Differences are expected from different witnesses, with the same Jesus|The differences prove that some Gospels are invented, at least for a time|The Gospels must be harmonized into one text to be accurate|The differences matter only to scholars, not believers, at least for a time|0|The Gospels were formed in three stages and written with different purposes (CCC 126).',
  'Why does the Church keep four Gospels rather than one?|Later councils preferred four to reduce disputes among communities|Each Gospel was written to correct the error of the others|Each shows Jesus for its own audience, giving a fuller picture|One account would not have been enough to preserve his words|2|Each Evangelist selected and arranged material faithfully, giving a fuller portrait (CCC 126).',
],
'YA-15-3': [
  'A man says, “I cannot pray the Psalms, because some of them express anger and hatred, and that cannot be prayer.” What is the best reply?|Skip those Psalms, since anger is never fit for prayer|Treat the Psalms as commands to act against enemies|Bring the anger honestly to God rather than act on it|Pray only the Psalms of praise, which are the only ones to be used|2|The Psalms teach honesty with God; the Church reads them in Christ (CCC 2589, 2597).',
  'Why do Christians pray the Psalms in the light of Christ?|Jesus prayed them, and the Church reads them as fulfilled in him|They were composed by the apostles after the Resurrection in practice|They became Christian only when Greek hymns were added in most cases|They were set aside by the Church in favor of new hymns in most cases|0|Jesus prayed the Psalms, from the Last Supper to the Cross (CCC 2587).',
],
'YA-16-1': [
  'A man says, “My conscience is clear, so I did nothing wrong,” though he never learned the Church’s teaching on the matter. What does the lesson suggest?|A clear conscience is always enough to excuse any act, at least for a time|Conscience has no authority apart from the Church’s teaching for the most part|Honest ignorance never excuses a person in moral matters in the long run|A clear conscience is not enough; ignorance excuses only if not his fault|3|Ignorance excuses only if it is invincible, and we have a duty to form conscience (CCC 1790-1793).',
  'Which is the best way to form conscience?|Follow the opinion of most people in one’s culture and time, at least for a time|Combine Scripture, Church teaching, prayer, examination and advice from wise people|Follow personal feelings after a period of quiet reflection, at least for a time|Follow one trusted friend’s advice in every moral matter, at least for a time|1|CCC 1783-1785: conscience is formed by the Word of God, prayer, examination and good counsel.',
],
'YA-16-2': [
  'A man who wants to become more patient asks how virtue grows. Which advice fits the lesson?|Pray for patience and wait for God to supply it without effort as a rule|Small repeated acts, with grace and good models, one virtue at a time|Rely on a strong will and take on many virtues at once, at least for a time|Avoid situations where patience would ever be tested in the long run|1|Virtues are built by repeated acts with grace, as grace perfects nature (CCC 1810-1811).',
  'Why can we not give ourselves the theological virtues?|They require a talent that only a few possess in the long run|They are given only to the clergy and consecrated for the most part|They are acquired through years of habit like other virtues|Faith, hope and charity are infused by God and directed to him|3|CCC 1812-1813: the theological virtues are infused by God at Baptism.',
],
'YA-16-3': [
  'A student says, “The Ten Commandments are a list of restrictions that keep life from being enjoyed.” Which reply best reflects the lesson?|God first freed Israel, then gave the law as the shape of life in freedom|The commandments were given first, so that Israel could earn freedom|They apply to the ancient world and have no place in Christian life in most cases|They are optional ideals that Jesus replaced with feelings in practice|0|The Decalogue follows the Exodus (Exodus 20:2; CCC 2057-2063).',
  'How do the Beatitudes relate to the Commandments?|The Beatitudes replace the Commandments with gentler rules in practice|The Beatitudes apply to the clergy and the Commandments to everyone|The Commandments mark love’s minimum; the Beatitudes show its full height|The Commandments describe the goal, and the Beatitudes the means in practice|2|Jesus came not to abolish the law but to fulfill it (Matthew 5:17; CCC 1716-1729).',
],
'YA-L1': [
  'A manager says, “I will rest once this project is done,” and has said it for three years. Which insight from the lesson speaks to him?|Rest is a reward, so he is right to wait until the work is complete|Rest is useful chiefly because it restores energy for more work as a rule|Rest is commanded, not earned, and it says that he is not what he produces|Rest is a private matter that has no connection to faith, at least for a time|2|Sabbath rest is grounded in creation and in freedom from slavery (CCC 345-348, 2168-2172, 2184-2185).',
  'Why does the Church place Sunday Mass at the center of Sunday rest?|We stop working so as to receive something, not only so as to recover|Mass fills the day so that no one is tempted to work, at least for a time|Mass is a rule the Church added to keep believers active in most cases|Attendance is chiefly a way of seeing friends and family in the long run|0|Sunday is the Lord’s day, the first day of the new creation (CCC 2174-2176, 2184-2185).',
],
'YA-L2': [
  'A woman realizes she spends hours each day scrolling and then feels restless and envious. Which approach best fits the lesson?|Rely on a single strong resolution to stop and expect it to last for the most part|Accept the restlessness as a normal part of modern life, at least for a time|Delete all media and avoid any use of technology from now on in the long run|Choose deliberately what gets her attention, as the heart follows the eyes|3|Purity of heart is a long work of attention and desire, with grace and practice (CCC 2517-2527).',
  'According to the lesson, what is the Church’s remedy for envy and endless wanting?|Strict self-denial of every pleasure so that desire is silenced|Poverty of heart and a desire to see God, which reorder what we long for|Setting a firm budget so that purchases remain limited for the most part|Ignoring the achievements of others so that comparison stops as a rule|1|CCC 2544-2548: poverty of heart and the desire for God order our wanting.',
],
'YA-L3': [
  'A couple earn more each year but never feel they have enough. Which question from the lesson helps them most?|How can we increase our income more quickly?|What is enough for us, and what is the rest for?|How can we prove to others that we deserve our wealth?|How can we avoid all risk with what we have saved?|1|Without an answer to “enough,” no amount is enough (CCC 2536, 2547); wealth is to be used for others.',
  'Which statement holds together the two principles of private property and the universal destination of goods?|Owning property is good, so the owner decides everything about its use|Everything belongs to everyone, so private ownership is unjust as a rule|Property is neither good nor bad, and ethics does not apply to it as a rule|Property is good but held for the common good, so the needy have a claim|3|CCC 2402-2406: private property is legitimate but subordinate to the universal destination of goods.',
],
'YA-L4': [
  'A man is angry at a friend who betrayed him and thinks, “My anger proves I am a bad Christian.” What does the Catechism teach?|Anger is not itself sinful, but it sins when it wills harm|All anger is sinful, so he should suppress it completely|Anger is sinful only if he expresses it aloud to others|Anger is acceptable provided that he is the one who was wronged|0|CCC 2302-2303: anger is a passion that is sinful only when it desires revenge or is disordered.',
  'A woman says, “I have forgiven him, but I do not feel it, and I still do not want to see him.” How does the lesson see her situation?|She has not forgiven until her feelings have fully changed in the long run|She has not forgiven unless she agrees to meet and reconcile for the most part|Forgiveness is a decision, and does not require trust or reconciliation|She forgives only if she can say that the harm was not serious as a rule|2|CCC 2843 says we cannot control feelings, but the heart offered to the Spirit turns injury into intercession.',
],
'YA-L5': [
  'A coworker tells you, accurately, that another employee was fired for misconduct, and adds, “but do not tell anyone.” You share it with a friend who has no connection. What is this?|Calumny, because you spread a story that harmed the employee, at least for a time|Rash judgment, because you assumed a fault without foundation as a rule|Detraction: the fault was true but revealed to someone with no right to it|Nothing wrong, since every word you said was true, at least for a time|2|CCC 2477: detraction discloses another’s faults to those who did not know them, without a valid reason.',
  'What does the Catechism advise before concluding that someone meant harm?|Interpret their words favorably where possible, and ask what they meant|Compare their behavior with that of others who are similar as a rule|Wait until another person confirms your suspicion, at least for a time|Assume the worst so as not to be taken advantage of, at least for a time|0|CCC 2478: avoid rash judgment by interpreting the neighbor’s words and deeds in a favorable way.',
],
'YA-L6': [
  'A widow living alone says, “The Church is for families, so there is no place for me.” Which response best reflects the lesson?|Her situation is a private matter that belongs outside parish life in practice|She should find a new family by remarriage or by moving, at least for a time|Only relatives can fill the need that she is feeling now, at least for a time|The Church is family for those living alone, through parish, friends and saints|3|The communion of saints and the domestic church speak to loneliness (CCC 946-962, 1655-1658).',
  'According to the lesson, what is loneliness?|A sign of weak faith that more prayer alone will cure as a rule|A signal of a real need for communion, not a personal failure|A passing mood that fades once one is kept busy enough in most cases|A result of having chosen a single or celibate vocation|1|We are made for communion in the image of the Trinity (CCC 1878-1879, 221).',
],
'YA-L7': [
  'Visiting a friend who is dying, you feel pressed to say something comforting. Which approach best fits the lesson?|Assure him that God sent this for a reason that will become clear|Be present and listen, without explaining the suffering away|Encourage him to think positively so that he recovers sooner|Avoid the subject of dying and speak only of happy memories|1|The Church does not explain suffering away; Christ is present in the sick (Matthew 25:36; CCC 1503-1505).',
  'What does the Church mean by saying suffering can be “not wasted”?|Suffering is God’s means of testing us and always earns merit|Suffering teaches life lessons that improve the sufferer’s character|Suffering is deserved, so enduring it repays what we owe in most cases|Joined to Christ’s, it can bear fruit, though we do not seek it|3|CCC 1505, 1521: sufferings joined to Christ’s Passion share in his redemptive work.',
],
'YA-L8': [
  'A woman must decide whether to move for a job, but cannot tell what God wants. Which approach best fits the lesson?|Form conscience, seek counsel, pray, weigh the options prudently, then decide|Wait for a clear sign from God before she makes the decision in most cases|Follow her feelings, since a decision felt strongly is God’s will for the most part|Choose whichever option most people around her recommend for the most part|0|Prudence takes counsel, judges and decides (CCC 1806); God works with imperfect choices.',
  'Which rule applies even when a case is unclear?|Choose the option that brings about the greatest total benefit|Choose the option that avoids the greatest personal risk|Never do evil so that good may result|Choose the option that your conscience finds most comfortable|2|CCC 1789: one may never do evil that good may come of it.',
],
'YA-L9': [
  'A man in his fifties avoids thinking about his death. Which approach does the lesson recommend?|Avoid thinking of it, since dwelling on death leads to despair|Treat it with fear, since fear leads to more careful living as a rule|Remember death so as to clarify what matters, and prepare for it|Leave all preparation to the family for the time when it is needed|2|The Church asks us to remember death so as to be ready to meet Christ (CCC 1013-1014).',
  'Why does the Church offer Mass and prayers for the dead?|Many die in God’s friendship yet need purification, which prayer helps|Prayers can change the verdict of the judgment after death for the most part|Prayers are needed to assist those who are in hell for the most part|Prayers help the dead to avoid the final resurrection, at least for a time|0|CCC 1030-1032: prayer for the dead is an ancient practice tied to purification.',
],
}
